\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real rallies as traders cheer more ambitious fiscal target\b0\par\par\uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2016\par 23:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates with final prices)\par     By Bruno Federowski\par     SAO PAULO, July 8 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real rallied by\par more than two percent on Friday as traders saw the government's\par more ambitious fiscal target for 2017 as a sign of interim\par President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michel Temer\f28 \uc2 \f2 's commitment to austerity.\par     Officials announced a primary deficit goal of 139 billion\par reais ($42 billion) for 2017, compared with this year's\par estimated gap of 170.5 billion reais. \par     Concerns that Temer's administration could sacrifice fiscal\par austerity for support in a divided Congress had contributed to\par weakening the real for the five previous trading days.\par     "The fiscal target represents a victory of the fiscal hawks\par inside the government, which is all the market wants to see,"\par said Leme Investimentos partner Paulo Petrassi.\par     The positive tone spilled into stock markets, with Brazil's\par benchmark Bovespa stock index rising for the third day\par in a row.\par     Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Est\u225\'00\'E1cio Participa\u231\'00\'E7\u245\'00\'F5es SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  were up 2.6\par percent, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kroton Educacional SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  advanced 4.1\par percent. Est\u225\'00\'E1cio's board is scheduled to meet on Thursday to\par discuss a sweetened bid from Kroton, the world's largest\par education company by market value.\par     The real advanced more than regional peers as\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  refrained from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after doing so for five straight sessions.\par     Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in general strengthened on Friday\par on a rebound in global risk appetite following\par stronger-than-expected U.S. jobs data. \par     The figures are the latest sign of recovery in the world's\par biggest economy, but traders bet they will not be enough to\par drive the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  to increase interest rates.\par     Fed policymakers have repeatedly said they will look for\par clarity over the economic fallout from Britain's decision to\par leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  before tightening policy.\par    \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 2128 GMT:\par  Stock indexes                                daily %    YTD %\par                                                change   change\par                                    Latest              \par  MSCI Emerging Markets                828.84     0.22     4.37\par  MSCI LatAm                          2259.47     2.65    23.48\par  Brazil Bovespa                     53140.74     0.77    22.59\par  Mexico IPC                         45744.29     0.77     6.44\par  Chile IPSA                          4024.96     0.68     9.37\par  Chile IGPA                         19898.49     0.71     9.62\par  Argentina MerVal                   14689.61    -0.01    25.82\par  Colombia IGBC                       9612.51    -0.53    12.46\par  Venezuela IBC                      11809.16     0.84   -19.05\par                                                               \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                    daily %    YTD %\par                                                change   change\par                                