\par\par \b \uc2 Coffee Gains on Strength of Brazilian  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2016\par 22:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Coffee prices ended higher on Friday, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil, the world's largest growing region, rose against the dollar for the first time in six sessions.\par \par \uc2 When Brazil's real strengthens against the dollar, it encourages producers to hold back on sales as they await more favorable exchange rates for their dollar-denominated goods.\par \par \uc2 Arabica coffee for September delivery rose 1.6% to close at $1.441 a pound on the ICE Futures U.S. exchange, the first rise for the most-active contract in four sessions.\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets bounced on Friday, as investors remained cautious about the prospect of U.S. interest-rate increases despite a stronger-than-expected jobs reports from the \uc2 U.S. Labor Department\uc2 . While the report shows nonfarm payrolls rose in June beyond what analysts had expected, unemployment continued to rise and adjustments to May's payroll figures showed the weakest month of hiring since 2010.\par \par \uc2 Brokers in Brazil say producers have been hesitant to sell as they are still comfortably working their way through sales made last year when price levels were more favorable. Last year, the real weakened to its lowest level against the dollar, encouraging producers to sell forward this year's crop. "The producers aren't in a hurry to sell coffee," said Thiago Marques Cazarini, a coffee broker with Cazarini Trading Company in Brazil. "Something must happen dramatically for everything to get rolling again."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cazarini said at current prices, the real would need to strike near 3.70 per dollar versus 3.2867 today, or New York futures would need to drop dramatically to about $1.20 per pound to worry producers enough to sell forward over fears of lower prices.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past week, suggesting they want stability near the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current levels, says Volcafe.\par \par \uc2 In other markets, raw sugar for October dropped 0.8% to close at 19.57 cents a pound, cocoa for September lost 0.4% to close at $3,097 a ton, frozen concentrated orange juice futures edged higher, up 1.1% to end at $1.8795 a pound and December cotton closed up 1% at 65.81 cents a pound.\par \par \uc2 -- Write to Julie Wernau at julie.wernau@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 08, 2016 16:00 ET (20:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160708ec780036j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
