\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading: Brazil Real Beats Havens After Brexit -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jon Sindreu   \par \uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2016\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C4\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 In the wake of Brexit, investors expected the traditional haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen and Swiss franc, to soar.\par \par \uc2 But it was the Brazilian real that really took off.\par \par \uc2 An unlikely haven, the real is up 3.9% against the U.S. dollar since Thursday, June 23, the day the British voted to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen is up a less impressive 2.9%, and the Swiss franc is actually 1.8% lower than a week earlier, in part due to the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from surging.\par \par \uc2 It isn't that investors have suddenly found a new haven in the politically unstable Brazil. There are many reasons behind the real's gains, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new swagger underscores how emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates weren't as badly affected by Brexit as many analysts expected.\par \par \uc2 That is partly because of the U.S. The uncertainty unleashed by Britain's vote pushed back expectations of a U.S. interest-rate increase. Investors tend to pull money out of emerging markets if they believe there will be higher interest interest rates -- and so higher returns -- in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which initially plunged after the results of the referendum became obvious early on the day after the U.K. vote, have also recovered. This suggests many emerging markets are now deemed to be broadly safe from any Brexit spillover.\par \par \uc2 Still, the resilience of the real is impressive. Even on that Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained slightly against the dollar. It is up 23% against the dollar this year so far, to 3.2138 reals per dollar late Thursday in New York, the highest since July 2015.\par \par \uc2 The real has risen so much that some analysts have been expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push it lower. Indeed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Thursday said it will hold an auction of reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap contracts on Friday, the first such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since the bank got a new president earlier this month. Reverse swap contracts have the effect of supporting the dollar against the real.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength has come despite Brazil's unstable political situation, with President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  suspended from office ahead of an impeachment trial in the country's Senate. The economy also remains in a deep recession.\par \par \uc2 Part of the real's strength can also be credited to its recent weakness. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost half of its value since 2011, when measured against the U.S. dollar, and hit an all-time low in January. So it has space to gain.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping interest rates high -- at 14.25% -- and economists expect inflation to keep easing, two positive factors for any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the recent rally is also a testament to how much many investors believe that once the current political regime is sidelined, Brazil's economic potential will be released.\par \par \uc2 "A somewhat more stable external environment, a government shift and prospects for sounder economic policies and fiscal adjustments have lowered Brazil's risk premium," said Alejandro Cuadrado, an analyst at Spanish bank \uc2 BBVA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Write to Jon Sindreu at jon.sindreu@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 01, 2016 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160701ec71000de\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
