\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Mexico peso seen most vulnerable in Latam to potential Fed rate hike\b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2016\par 14:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * For poll data://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/\b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2 -polls\par \par \uc2 By Gabriel Burin and Silvio Cascione\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES/BRASILIA, June 2 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably face months of renewed volatility as a challenging global outlook weighs on the Mexican peso and a lingering political crisis burdens the Brazilian real, a Reuters poll showed on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Unease over the prospect of a U.S. interest rate hike in coming months helped send the Mexican peso down 7.5 percent in against the dollar in May. It will take one year to slowly recover to levels last seen one month ago, according to forecasts of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to trade at 17.50 in 12 months. This estimate was 1.4 percent weaker than last month's projection for the same period, representing the steepest cut in forecasts among Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the survey.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, strategists were slightly more bullish than a month ago on the real, the Peruvian sol and the Colombian and Argentine pesos.\par \par \uc2 The real was seen at 3.82 reais to the dollar in one year, 2.1 percent stronger than in last month's poll. The poll also saw the Colombian and the Argentine pesos, at 2,960 and 16.50, respectively, in 12 months' time, and the Peruvian sol at 3.50 to the dollar in the same time period.\par \par \uc2 Analysts were slightly more bearish than a month ago on the Chilean peso, seeing it at 690 one year from now.\par \par \uc2 Overall, traders in the region will keep a watchful eye on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "The impact of Fed rate hikes is hard to predict, increasing risk aversion and a flight to the safety of the dollar," said Felipe Campos, an economist at the Alianza brokerage in Bogota.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take into account the exchange rate and the Fed's upcoming rates decision when it meets on June 30 to set monetary policy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Agustin Carstens said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 A sharp drop in Mexico's peso pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time since 2009 and to raise interest rates unexpectedly after an unannounced meeting.\par \par \uc2 Now, the effectiveness of any renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  move could be limited, said Pedro Tuesta, an economist with research firm 4Cast.\par \par \uc2 "The problem for \uc2 Banxico\uc2  (the \uc2 Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only works when liquidity is low and so far that is not the case," he said. With the peso trading the largest volumes in Latin American markets, "\uc2 Banxico\uc2  will have to use heavy artillery", Tuesta added.\par \par \uc2 Investors need to pay attention to other central banks too.\par \par \uc2 According to analysts with \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , the biggest threat to the Mexican peso and other emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not come from the Fed, which has tried to communicate every policy step in order to avoid market turmoil.\par \par \uc2 "In the last 18 months, the biggest moves (of emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) have come from poorly handled policy shifts by the SNB, BOJ, and the PBoC," wrote the analysts led by Vincent Chaigneau in a report referring to the Swiss, Japanese and Chinese central banks.\par \par \uc2 As Mexico bolsters its hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cushion with an extended credit line from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , data from the \uc2 U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2  showed hedge funds increasing their bets against the peso.\par \par \uc2 Leveraged funds had a net position of 36,634 short contracts for the peso as of May 24, the highest since February's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Positions for \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real told another story, with 16,987 net long contracts, not far from this year's record.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has steadied around the 3.60 mark after interim President \uc2 Michel Temer\uc2  pledged to reduce the country's massive deficit.\par \par \uc2 However, 12 strategists in the poll said the real could suffer from political uncertainty after two ministers of Temer's Cabinet had to step aside after leaked recordings suggested they worked to halt a corruption probe.\par \par \uc2 (For other stories from the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll: ) (Additional reporting by Nelson Bocanegra in Bogota, Jean Luis Arce and Noe Torres in Mexico City, Ursula Scollo in Lima, Anthony Esposito and Felipe Iturrieta in Santiago, Hernan Nessi in Buenos Aires; Editing by Ross Finley and W Simon)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-5T12:16:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160602ec6200z15\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
