\par\par \b \uc2 Brazilian Real Strengthens as Senate Votes on Impeachment; Shares Fall -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2016\par 23:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Jeffrey T. Lewis and Rogerio Jelmayer \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 S\u195\'00\'C3O PAULO -- The Brazilian real strengthened against the dollar Wednesday as the country's Senate prepared to vote on whether to hold an impeachment trial for President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dilma Rousseff\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and shares declined.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Ibovespa stocks index fell 0.6% to 52764 points. The real exited active trading at 3.4466 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, after closing at 3.4702 on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The Senate is expected to spend most of the day debating whether Ms. Rousseff should be tried. She is accused of violating budget laws by delaying payments owed to state-controlled banks. Her detractors say that amounted to illegally forcing the lenders to grant credit to the government. Ms. Rousseff has denied the accusations.\par \par \uc2 If the Senate votes to hold a trial for the president, she will have to step aside for up to 180 days while the trial takes place. Vice President Michel Temer would assume the presidency during her suspension. In the event that she is ousted, he would serve out the remainder of her term, which runs through the end of 2018.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian financial markets have been reacting positively to the outlook for a change in government because \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy has foundered under Ms. Rousseff's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and heavy spending on subsidies, which have led to growing budget deficits and rising public debt. Brazil's gross domestic product contracted 3.8% last year and is expected to shrink at a similar pace this year.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are getting ready for a country under Temer's management, which will put much more emphasis on fiscal control" than Ms. Rousseff's government, said Alex Agostini, chief economist at local rating agency Austin Rating. "That fiscal effort, if it's adopted now, would pave the way for a return to growth."\par \par \uc2 The stock market opened higher on Wednesday, then slowly gave up ground over the course of the day, pulled lower by a decline in U.S. markets and by some uncertainty over who Mr. Temer might put in his cabinet, according to Paulo Figueiredo, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the FN Capital brokerage in Petropolis.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of today's vote has already been priced in by the stock market," he said.\par \par \uc2 The real has strengthened about 15% from the start of this year through Wednesday, and the Ibovespa gained almost 22% in the same period. Both stand to gain more if the Senate votes to hold a trial for Ms. Rousseff, according to Pedro Paulo Silveira, chief economist at the Nova Futura brokerage in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo.\par \par \uc2 "If the Senate votes the way everyone expects, it will open the way for more gains," he said. "The risk that she won't be removed will be smaller, and if that happens, then investors will want more Brazilian assets."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts, which help support the dollar against the real, for weeks. Analysts say the goal has been to keep the real from strengthening past about 3.5 to the dollar. If Mr. Temer steps in for Ms. Rousseff, he is expected to replace \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2 , and that could lead to less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the bank, Mr. Silveira said.\par \par \uc2 "The bank would be much more focused on fighting inflation and shrinking the deficit" than on defending the real, he said.\par \par \uc2 In the stock market, state-controlled lender \uc2 Banco do Brasil SA\uc2  fell 4.5% to 20.48 reais, one of the biggest declines in the Ibovespa on Wednesday. Meat-packer JBS SA retreated 2.5% to 8.71 reais and aircraft manufacturer \uc2 Embraer SA\uc2  gave up 2.4% to 19.18 reais.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jeffrey T. Lewis at jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com and Rogerio Jelmayer at rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 11, 2016 17:10 ET (21:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160511ec5b00452\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
