\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Revived  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Swap Sales Won't Help  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Much: Capital Economics -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Teresa Rivas   \par \uc2 504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2015\par 20:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken a beating of late, and the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  just announced that it would auction \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps Wednesday and Thursday, a resumption of a program begun and dropped earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Capital Economics' Chief Emerging Markets Economist Neil Shearing notes that the move comes as \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to stabilize its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without using more conventional \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  like selling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, but the impact on the real has been "muted" thus far. Previously, Brazil had sold more than $90 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps from April 2013 through March of this year, when it halted the program.\par \par \uc2 Shearing writes that there are three main takeaways from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to restart the sales:\par \par \uc2 First, the size of the planned auctions is reasonably large. Up to $2bn in swaps will be auctioned in total, with auctions so far planned for today and tomorrow (with additional dates presumably added in the future). To put this into context, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first launched the programme in 2013 the weekly limit on the auctions was $2bn, but by the end it was auctioning less than $200mn a week.\par \par \uc2 Second, we're not convinced restarting the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap programme will do much to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Indeed, if anything, the initial reaction has been for the real to fall further (it is currently down by 1.8% against the dollar today). \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market rarely have much effect and there are particular issues with using \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps as a policy tool. Specifically, if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gets caught on the wrong side of the trade (i.e. the real weakens further), the government will incur a fiscal cost. Losses on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts have already inflated the government's budget deficit by more than 1% of GDP this year, adding to concerns about the fiscal outlook.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (and government) is clearly becoming spooked by the fall in the real. Generally speaking, while many analysts have worried about the impact of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we have taken the view that it is part of the solution rather than the problem. If \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls become disorderly, however, that complicates the issue for policymakers. It's early days, but if the real continues to come under pressure it's possible that the interest rate tightening cycle could be re-started at next month's COPOM meeting (20/21st October).\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the Brazilian real closed at its weakest level ever against the dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The iShares MSCI   Brazil Capped exchange-traded fund ( EWZ) was down \par more than 3% in recent trading, while the   the \f28 \uc2 \f2 iShares Latin America 40 ETF\f28 \uc2 \f2  ( ILF) was losing  2.7% and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 iShares MSCI Emerging Markets ETF\f28 \uc2 \f2  ( EEM) was down 1.3%. \par  \par  More at Barron's Emerging Markets blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 23, 2015 14:36 ET (18:36 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150923eb9n002h7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
