\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Rousseff, Cabinet Meet as Downgrade Deepens Economic Turmoil\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan   \par \uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2015\par 20:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO--Brazilian President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  called an emergency cabinet meeting Thursday after a sovereign-debt downgrade sent the real reeling and left the government scrambling to address the nation's fast-deteriorating economy.\par \par \uc2 Ratings firm \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  cut Brazil's debt rating to junk-grade BB+ in a surprise move late Wednesday that highlighted mounting political challenges to the government's efforts to correct a widening deficit. \uc2 S&P\uc2  maintained a negative outlook on the rating, indicating at least a one-in-three chance of another downgrade.\par \par \uc2 The move sent Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its weakest level against the dollar since late 2002 in early trading, prompting an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; a selloff in Brazilian bonds and heavy losses in shares of state-controlled companies including oil giant \uc2 Petr\u243\'00\'F3leo Brasileiro SA\uc2 , known as \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 . Analysts said that a downgrade had been partly built into Brazilian asset prices but that the timing--barely six weeks after \uc2 S&P\uc2  put Brazil's rating on negative watch--rang the alarm bells.\par \par \uc2 "After such a sudden change like the one carried out by \uc2 S&P\uc2 , it's going to be hard to stay calm in the coming days," said Jankiel Santos, chief economist at Haitong investment bank in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Low commodity prices are hammering Brazilian exports of iron ore and other raw materials. The sliding home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sent inflation soaring, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise its benchmark interest rate, the Selic, to 14.25%, one of the highest benchmark rates in the world. To combat falling revenue and rising debt, the government has raised taxes and cut some spending, steepening the economy's decline.\par \par \uc2 Central to \uc2 S&P\uc2 's decision was a perception of wavering commitment from within Ms. Rousseff's government to balance its books.\par \par \uc2 Congress has repeatedly rejected program cuts and tax increases as a way to shore up Brazil's deteriorating finances. That has forced Ms. Rousseff's economic team to backtrack its 2015 fiscal targets. Last week, it presented a 2016 budget to Congress that forecast a deficit before interest payments equivalent to 0.5% of Brazil's gross domestic product, down from a projected 2% primary surplus early in the year. It was the first time in Brazil's history that an administration admitted its inability to control its own accounts.\par \par \uc2 "The change in strategy on the budget proposal was key for us," said Lisa Schineller, a managing director in the sovereign ratings department for \uc2 S&P\uc2  in a conference call on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's economy is suffering its worst recession in a quarter-century, forecast to shrink 2.4% this year and 0.5% in 2016. But policy makers' ability to support growth remains hamstrung by an inflation rate that is more than double the official target and a widening fiscal deficit that most economists say demands some combination of spending cuts and tax increases.\par \par \uc2 The proposed austerity measures have bred deep resentment toward Ms. Rousseff, whose popularity has been further dented by a corruption scandal at \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 . The president broke with tradition Monday by not making a televised address to the nation on Brazil's independence holiday.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil has a faltering economy and faces a severe political crisis," London-based Oxford Economics said in a note. "Challenges have continued to mount, weighing on the government's ability and willingness to submit a 2016 budget to Congress consistent with what President Dilma has signaled earlier in her second term."\par \par \uc2 At a news conference last week, the president addressed the conflicting views within her cabinet.\par \par \uc2 "In a family, we all know, there are varying opinions. The fact of having opinion A, B, C, of mom, dad and whoever, doesn't mean that the family is falling apart," Ms. Rousseff said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paul Kiernan at paul.kiernan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This was corrected at 3:22 p.m. ET because the original incorrectly spelled \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 's name in the headline.\par \par \uc2 10 Sep 2015 15:20 ET  Correction to Brazil's President, Cabinet Meet as Downgrade Deepens Economic Turmoil\par \par \uc2 "Brazil's President, Cabinet Meet as Downgrade Deepens Economic Turmoil, " at 2:21 p.m. ET, incorrectly spelled \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 's name in the headline. (9/10/15)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 10, 2015 15:20 ET (19:20 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150910eb9a003c0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
