\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  More Likely to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  Amid Selloff in Real -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 183 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2015\par 14:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:59 EDT - The Brazil real is the big loser in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the credit downgrade. The US dollar is up 2.8% at 3.8870 real. Strategists at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  say in a worst case scenario real could tumble to 4.14 per dollar at the end of this year. Still, the strategists believe \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not tap the rate-hike tool to prop up the bleeding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and instead turn to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Any hike by the BCB to limit BRL depreciation would be offset by a worsening of debt dynamics and an increase in Brazil's default probability," they say. "Monetary policy is reaching a point at which its effectiveness is falling considerably." The strategists add that although "Brazilian assets will certainly overshoot their fair values in the short term, a loss of Brazil's investment grade was considerably priced in by CDS and rates markets." (min.zeng@wsj.com;@minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 10, 2015 08:59 ET (12:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150910eb9a0020x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
