\par\par \uc2 Latin America News\par \b \uc2 Brazil Economy Plunges in Second Quarter\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan   \par \uc2 961 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2015\par 14:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO\u8212\'20\'14Brazil has entered its deepest economic downturn since the global financial meltdown of 2008-09, official data confirmed Friday.\par \par \uc2 But unlike the previous crisis, economists say the current problems were caused by errant economic policy at home and that the road to recovery will be a long one.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's gross domestic product shrank 1.9% between April and June from the previous three months in seasonally adjusted terms, the \uc2 Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics\uc2  said on Friday. It was the second consecutive quarter of decline after the economy shrank a revised 0.7% in January-March, meeting the criteria for what most economists consider a recession.\par \par \uc2 For a country that once foresaw itself ascending into the ranks of the world's five largest economies, the downturn represents a troubling setback. Per-capita incomes have been on the decline since 2014, after rising for the better part of a decade of heady government spending. Souring investor appetite for local assets has caused Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, to lose a quarter of its value so far this year.\par \par \uc2 A contraction was expected for the second quarter. Brazil's industrial production, stagnant for years, fell sharply in the first half of 2015, as demand for cars and other durable goods dried up. The construction sector has been paralyzed by a corruption investigation that has ensnared its biggest players. Retail sales have retreated, as consumer confidence has fallen to record lows, prompting the biggest quarterly drop in private consumption since 2001.\par \par \uc2 But most forecasters had expected the latest quarterly downturn to be more moderate. Bill Adams, a senior international economist at Pittsburgh-based \uc2 PNC Financial Services Group\uc2 , said the April-June result was "astoundingly bad." \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 ' Marcelo Carvalho quipped that Brazil's second-quarter domestic product was "gross."\par \par \uc2 What's less clear is when the bleeding will stop. Low commodity prices are hammering Brazilian exports of iron ore and other raw materials. The sliding home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sent inflation soaring, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise its benchmark interest rate, the Selic, to 14.25%, one of the highest benchmark rates in the world. To combat falling revenue and rising debt, the government has raised taxes and cut some spending, steepening the economy's decline.\par \par \uc2 "It's more like death from a thousand cuts than a big bubble in real estate or something," said Pedro Barbosa, a partner at Rio de Janeiro-based hedge fund STK Capital. "But now we have a long recession coming our way."\par \par \uc2 Experts say rising commodity prices during much of the last decade allowed Brazilian authorities to get away with what they characterize as shortsighted policy and fiscal profligacy. Under pressure to raise living standards in one of the world's most economically inequitable countries, policy makers tried to stimulate consumption by dishing out consumer loans, reducing interest rates and cutting taxes on goods like cars and appliances.\par \par \uc2 "The vast majority of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's problems were created at home, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, the growth of the state, the lack of reform," said Edwin Gutierrez, who manages emerging-market debt at $483 billion \uc2 Aberdeen Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many economists contend that largess came at the expense of investment, in which Brazil badly trails other large developing countries. As supply struggled to keep up with red-hot demand in recent years, inflation constantly hovered near the 6.5% upper limit of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range.\par \par \uc2 But instead of tightening the spigots on the money supply, Brazilian authorities combated the price pressures by subsidizing most of the goods and services under its control\u8212\'20\'14from electricity bills to fuel prices to bus fares. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fought \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation with a complicated mechanism that, according to one calculation by local newspaper Folha de S. Paulo, cost the government more than the country's flagship welfare program.\par \par \uc2 The measures proved unsustainable. State-controlled oil giant Petr\u243\'00\'F3 leo Brasileiro SA burned an estimated 60 billion reais ($17 billion) on gasoline and diesel subsidies for consumers between 2011 and 2014, driving its debt to the oil industry's highest.\par \par \uc2 In the end, the economic distortions became so great that Brazil's left-leaning President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  had to replace her top economic aide with a private-sector banker who abruptly loosened price controls and reversed other unconventional policies. But as a result, inflation soared to 9.6% as of mid-August. Economists project GDP will shrink by as much as 2.1% this year and an additional 0.2% in 2016, in what would be Brazil's first two-year contraction since 1930-31.\par \par \uc2 All of that comes after Brazil's economy posted annual growth of 4%, on average, between 2003 and 2013.\par \par \uc2 "What we're seeing now is a real reality check for Brazil, because we're seeing how it managed to sustain such high rates of growth during the commodities boom," said Edward Glossop, emerging-markets economist at London-based Capital Economics. "Now those props have been taken away. We're getting to the stage where Brazil will have growth of a very modest, low rate in the long term."\par \par \uc2 Adding to the uncertainty is the fallout from the wide corruption scandal surrounding \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 . Ms. Rousseff, with a public-approval rating of just 8%, is isolated and weak. And with dozens of lawmakers under investigation in the \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  scandal, Congress is disarray and all but gridlocked.\par \par \uc2 To restore robust growth, economists say Brazil needs to tackle deep-rooted problems like a Byzantine tax system, bloated public sector and stifling bureaucracy that delay infrastructure projects, suck up resources and smother innovation.\par \par \uc2 The current political turmoil in Bras\u237\'00\'ED lia dims prospects for such reforms, experts say. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120150828eb8s0006p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
