\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Emerging assets hit new multi-year lows on China contagion\b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2015\par 21:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Latin American markets, Sao Paulo dateline)\par \par \uc2 By Sujata Rao and Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 LONDON/SAO PAULO, Aug 24 (Reuters) - Contagion from China's equity malaise spread across emerging markets on Monday, driving \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multi-year lows against the dollar while a benchmark equity index slumped more than 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Concern over the world's No. 2 economy pushed Chinese mainland stocks into their biggest one-day fall since February 2007. They lost more than 8 percent and are now in the red for 2015 .\par \par \uc2 Selling escalated across emerging markets, taking MSCI's benchmark equity index to six-year lows in its biggest one-day loss since September 2011.\par \par \uc2 Regulators' futile efforts to stem the equity rout have raised doubts about Beijing's ability to deal with the crisis. Meanwhile investors fled markets such as Philippines and India, which have been relatively resilient.\par \par \uc2 "This 2015 emerging market crisis has the potential to become worse than the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis of 1997/98 as it is impacting global equity markets across the globe," said Bernd Berg, a strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Describing the day as a black Monday for Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Berg advised clients to short the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Chile, Brazil, and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar fell to six-year and 12-year lows, respectively. Mexico's peso slumped to an all-time low while the country's IPC stock index fell to its lowest since March 2014.\par \par \uc2 The South African rand hit an all-time low, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to say it may act to quell volatility.\par \par \uc2 Other emerging market officials also considered whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a member of President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 's economic team indicated that the government would continue to sell \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and, perhaps, dollars with repurchase agreements. For now, Brazil has ruled out selling dollars from its foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan, on the other hand, is "aggressively evaluating" the possibility of a large government fund to buy stocks, which plunged to 3-year lows.\par \par \uc2 Russia's economy minister said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should consider pausing its rate cutting cycle next month after the rouble slumped to eight-month lows.\par \par \uc2 "Policymakers in emerging markets will move in that direction (of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), this always happens in this environment. The question is: will they be successful?" said Maarten-Jan Bakkum, investment strategist for emerging markets at NN Investments in The Hague.\par \par \uc2 Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to multi-month lows but weathered the storm better than their Asian and Latin American peers as most are commodity importers and are more tied to the euro zone than to China. (Additional reporting by Gabriel Burin in Buenos Aires; Editing by Frances Kerry) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-24T20:13:19.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150824eb8o00mp8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
