\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam markets fall on China data, commodity prices\b0\par\par\uc2 502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2015\par 19:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SAO PAULO, Aug 3 (Reuters) - Latin American financial\par markets fell on Monday after disappointing factory data from\par China weighed on the price of the region's commodity exports. \par     Nearly every \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the region weakened against the\par dollar, with the Brazilian real falling as low as 3.46\par per dollar, its weakest level in over 12 years. All major stock\par markets sank, with the broader MSCI Latin American stock index\par  wiping out the previous session's gains.\par     China's factory activity shrank more than initially\par estimated in July as new orders fell, according to purchasing\par managers' data released on Monday. \par     China is Brazil's top trade partner and a key market for\par Latin American commodity exports such as iron-ore, soybeans,\par copper and petroleum.\par     Prices for most of those commodities fell, with copper\par  sinking to its weakest in six years and crude oil \par posting its biggest one-day drop in nearly a month.\par     The Colombian peso posted the biggest decline in the\par region, dropping about 1.4 percent. Petroleum is Colombia's top\par export and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tends to move in tandem with oil prices.\par     The Chilean peso, which is closely linked to copper\par prices, dropped about 1.2 percent.\par     Traders said part of the decline in the real came after the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday maintained the pace at which it\par rolls over expiring swap options, a signal that it will not step\par up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     In equities markets, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Bovespa stock index \par dropped its most in over a week, dragged down by shares of\par lender \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Bradesco SA\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     The bank on Monday said it agreed to buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings Plc\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par  Brazilian unit for 17.6 billion reais ($5.2 billion), a\par price far above what most investors were expecting.\par  \par     Chile's IPSA stock index dropped on a decline in\par banking shares, while Mexico's IPC stock index eased 0.25\par percent lower as mining shares fell. \par     \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1703 GMT:\par     \par  Stock indexes                        daily %    YTD %\par                              Latest    change   change\par  MSCI Emerging Markets        889.26    -1.38    -5.71\par                                                \par  MSCI LatAm                  2258.72       -2   -15.51\par                                                \par  Brazil Bovespa           50154.7659     -1.4     0.29\par                                    3           \par  Mexico IPC                 44640.75    -0.25     3.47\par                                                \par  Chile IPSA                  3825.26    -1.16    -0.67\par                                                \par  Chile IGPA                 18649.67    -0.98    -1.17\par                                                \par  Argentina MerVal          10934.819    -1.49    27.46\par                                                \par  Colombia IGBC               9913.78    -1.31   -14.79\par                                                \par  Venezuela IBC              15609.29     0.89   304.52\par                                