\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil readies a steep interest rate rise to salvage credibility\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alonso Soto   \par \uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2015\par 07:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, April 29 (Reuters) - Brazil is poised to deliver another big interest rate increase on Wednesday as the government tries to convince investors it is committed to taming high inflation, despite the risk of recession.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 9-member monetary policy committee is widely expected to raise the Selic rate 50 basis points - the fourth straight increase since December - to 13.25 percent, the highest in six years.\par \par \uc2 The Selic towers above the interest rates of fellow emerging economies India and Turkey, both at 7.5 percent. While those countries and other major economies have cut rates to shore up growth, Brazil has raised its rate 175 basis points in just six months.\par \par \uc2 Only six of the 48 analysts polled by Reuters last week believe the bank will increase the rate by 25 basis points.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is spearheading efforts by President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  to salvage credibility with investors after years of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and lavish spending jacked up prices and threatened \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's investment grade rating.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sharply criticized for bringing interest rates to a record low of 7.25 percent in 2012 to bolster a slow-moving economy, despite pressure on prices.\par \par \uc2 Now, aided by aggressive fiscal tightening, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has promised to bring 12-month inflation to the official target of 4.5 percent by 2016 from 8.13 percent in March.\par \par \uc2 "We believe the board will not disappoint the market with a smaller hike, particularly following its hawkish communications," \uc2 Joao Pedro Ribeiro\uc2 , an analyst with \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2 , said in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 Ribeiro, who forecasts a 50-basis-point increase, said high inflation expectations outweigh the positive effects of a stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2  and other directors repeated recently they will remain vigilant. They said past monetary tightening had not been enough to bring inflation back to the middle of the target range of 2.5 percent to 6.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not signaled when it will stop raising rates, but most analysts believe Wednesday's increase could be the last this year.\par \par \uc2 A near 9 percent appreciation of the real in April and the diminishing effects of steep increases in regulated prices are expected to ease inflation. Fears of a deeper economic recession this year are also expected to bring the cycle of increases to an end.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the Selic rate to end the year at 13.25 percent, but believe policymakers will cut it to 11.50 percent by late 2016, according to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey of economists released on Monday. (Reporting by Alonso Soto. Editing by Andre Grenon) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-4-29T06:00:00.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150429eb4t007ey\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
