\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Consumer Confidence Hit Fresh Record Low -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,033 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 12:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7:15 EDT - A day after the \uc2 Getulio Vargas Foundation\uc2  said Brazil industrial-sector confidence hit a fresh 6-year low this month, it hit a record low on the consumer side. The reading fell to 82.9 from 85.4 in February. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:12 EDT - Markets are struggling with some competing signals out of the eurozone. On the one hand, Germany is booming--or so the latest business-sentiment data suggest. But on the other, the Greece problem is looking increasingly dicey as the ECB told the country's shaky banks to stop expanding their holdings of Greek sovereign debt. If it weren't for Greece, which is likely a factor in the weak trading so far seen in European equities, global investors would likely be in a better mood as a retraction in the dollar had helped to underscore a modest pickup in risk appetites. Shares in Asia and elsewhere were mostly buoyed overnight, and a surprise move by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program didn't prompt any panic selling of the real or competing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:07 EDT - A broken record, but yet-another well-below-consensus quarterly report from irrigation-equipment maker Lindsay (LNN). The F2Q results include a surprise revenue drop as equipment revenue slumped 20%, including 27% domestically as farmers continue to curtail spending amid low commodity prices. But LNN's infrastructure revenue nearly doubled from a year earlier. While the company's irrigation business has struggled, LNN hasn't been sitting on the sidelines. It made a $70M acquisition in January of machine-to-machine technology company Elecsys and bought some $90M of stock the past year. LNN, whose market cap is just under $1B, is inactive premarket and down 8.7% this month. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:00 EDT - "As the conclusion of refinery-maintenance season approaches, margins are likely to recede from 1Q's very high levels" in the space, says Cowen. "However, based on performance during and after previous periods of unsustainably high margins, we see opportunity for well-positioned independent refiners to maintain longer-term share price outperformance." The investment bank broadly boosts EPS views for this quarter among refiners. Its favorites continue to include Valero (VLO), Marathon (MPC), Western (WNR), PBF and Delek (DK). (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 6:46 EDT - South African President \uc2 Jacob Zuma\uc2  highlights the ongoing need for "economic liberation" at the first Black Industrialist Indaba in Johannesburg. Empowering black entrepreneurs and industrialists "is not racism in reverse. It is uprooting and getting rid of racism." He adds the push reflects a need to broaden ownership, management and control of the economy to the black majority in South Africa. The president cited Brazil, Malaysia, Japan and South Korea as offering "significant insight on the journey of industrialization," and said the government's focus on boosting the manufacturing sector is reflective of its lack of development. Some companies operating in South Africa have said the government's black empowerment policies, have created a more high-risk investing environment as compliance is difficult due to a dearth of technically skilled black workers. (alexandra.wexler@wsj.com; @alexandrawexler)\par \par \uc2 6:44 EDT - The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since August 2013 through its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap auction program, ostensibly to help curtail volatility. But letting that effort expire at month's end might itself prompt less volatility, says Sidnei Nehme of brokerage NGO. He notes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue rolling over old contracts, which are equivalent to selling dollars in the futures market. Nehme expects dollar to trade at BRL3.1-3.2 this year; it's wrapped up Tuesday at BRL3.125. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 6:34 EDT - It's been widely assumed that as the Fed's first rate hike in nearly a decade looms, stock-market volatility would increase. RBC says that may not end up happening. The VIX has already pulled back from a year-end level of 19.2 to 13.6. "Our work shows that volatility moves with the economic cycle, drifting lower as the expansion matures. Importantly, we believe recessionary risks remain muted. This should result in a lower level of volatility." It was historically low last year, and more declines are poised "to be one of several factors supporting higher stock prices." (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 6:17 EDT - Merging Heinz with Kraft (KRFT) is one way for 3G Capital and \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway\uc2  (BRKB) to notch some income from their $23B purchase 2 years ago of Heinz--even without selling down any of their stake. The combined company vows to maintain KRFT's current dividend payout--55c/share quarterly--after closing. There's currently 588M KRFT shares outstanding, so there's going to be some 1.2B after the merger. At the current dividend rate, that's $1.3B annually for to-be-51%-owners 3G and Berkshire. Meanwhile, some $1.5B of cost savings/year are anticipated, though the deal isn't likely to boost the combined firm's bottom line until 2017; the deal is seen closing by the end of this year. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 6:08 EDT - Heinz and Kraft (KRFT) have in fact inked a merger pact, a deal which would marry two iconic US food brands as the companies look to muddle through a soft demand environment stateside and stoke growth overseas. The Kraft Heinz Co. would be the 5th-biggest food-and-beverage firm globally, they say, with $28B of annual revenue and 8 billion-dollar brands. KRFT shareholders would own 49% of the combined firm and get what is being called a "special dividend" of $16.50/share at closing. Berkshire (BRKB) and 3G Capital will make that $10B payment, which will also help give current Heinz owners slight majority control. KRFT jumps 16% premarket to $71. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 25, 2015 07:15 ET (11:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150325eb3p00180\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
