\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  unlikely to keep  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  for very long -source\b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 19:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief)\par \par \uc2 By Alonso Soto and Luciana Otoni\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, March 18 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has little room to continue with its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap program because it is running out of ammunition to counter a weakening real, a senior government official told Reuters on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The weaker real is a key factor supporting the economy, which could very well contract this year as investments dwindle, said the official, who is directly involved in economic policymaking but declined to be identified because he was not authorized to speak publicly.\par \par \uc2 "They (at the bank) don't have many options to continue with the swaps policy for very long," said the official.\par \par \uc2 He added that the bank has already sold the equivalent of 40 percent of its foreign reserves, which is weighing on its credibility with the market to continue effectively battling a weaker real.\par \par \uc2 The swap program, which started in August 2013, is scheduled to end on March 31, but investors believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could opt to again reduce the scope of the program in a new extension.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2 , said later Wednesday that the swap program has a "significant size," hinting that the daily sale of swaps could be reduced or even halted.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty over the continuation of the program helped the real soften in recent weeks, making it the worst performer among 152 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The real erased early losses and gained 0.15 percent on Wednesday afternoon after the U.S. Federal Reserve signaled it could wait longer than investors expected to raise interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The program provides investors with a daily supply of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, derivatives that offer protection against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment for this story.\par \par \uc2 The senior official also said the government plans to freeze about 60 billion reais ($18 billion) in expenditures this year in an effort to reach its key fiscal savings goal. The freeze is part of an annual fiscal exercise designed to limit inflation and signal the government's commitment to austerity.\par \par \uc2 Last year the government froze about 40 billion reais.\par \par \uc2 "We need to make a strong adjustment in our accounts, tighten the belt," said the official. ($1 = 3.2590 Brazilian reais) (Additional reporting by Flavia Bohone; Editing by Jeffrey Benkoe) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-3-18T19:56:13.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150318eb3i00m36\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
