\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  will not step up  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  to buoy  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  -sources\b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 00:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from other sources, context)\par \par \uc2 By Alonso Soto and Luciana Otoni\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, March 13 (Reuters) - Brazilian authorities will not bow to market pressure to increase support for the real despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp sell-off on Friday, four government sources told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The real weakened nearly 4 percent on Friday, hitting 3.28 per dollar, its weakest level since April 2003, due a surge of the dollar abroad and amid growing political uncertainty stemming from a corruption scandal at state-run oil company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many investors expected President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  to step up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily sale of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps - derivatives that provide investors with protection against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses.\par \par \uc2 But officials told Reuters Brazil will not use its international reserves to halt the real's depreciation, which could ultimately help its exports at a time when the economy is contracting.\par \par \uc2 "It would be useless to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said an official who asked not to be named to speak freely. "This is a global trend. The dollar is stronger everywhere."\par \par \uc2 The real has weakened 13 percent in March, making it the worst performer among 152 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The decision not to step into the market signals a major policy shift for Rousseff, who during her first term in office increased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  initially to weaken an overly appreciated real and later to halt it plunge.\par \par \uc2 Friday's sharp depreciation of the real was also explained by investors' seeking a new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  level needed to correct Brazil's economic imbalances, a finance ministry source said.\par \par \uc2 The official said the market was testing the government and Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  overshoot was not the result of capital flight, which would merit direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both the finance ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment for this story.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap program is due to expire on March 31. The bank has not said if it will continue with the program that started in 2013, but has slowed roll-over pace of swaps.\par \par \uc2 The government believes the negative impact a weaker real would have on inflation will be offset later this year by its fiscal and monetary tightening, a senior lawmaker who was briefed by members of Rousseff's economic team told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "The government is willing to pay the price of higher inflation in the short term because it is confident prices will start to subside in the third quarter," said the lawmaker from Rousseff's Workers' Party.\par \par \uc2 A weaker real makes imports more expensive, raising price pressures when inflation surged to 10-year highs in February. (Reporting by Luciana Otoni; Writing Alonso Soto; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama, Andrew Hay and Diane Craft) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-3-14T00:22:28.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150313eb3d00mb0\par }