\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil central bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention \highlight\uc2  may continue past March -source\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patricia Duarte   \par \uc2 190 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2015\par 21:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Feb 3 (Reuters) - \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no plans to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, which it may extend beyond March, a senior member of the government's economic team told Reuters on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Some traders speculated that the bank could wind down its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Finance Minister Joaquim Levy suggested on Friday the government had no intention of keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stronger than the market would naturally dictate.\par \par \uc2 "There's nothing to that ... It was a bad comment on a bad day," the source said on condition of anonymity.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell 3 percent against the dollar on Friday and slipped another 1 percent on Monday to near its lowest in a decade, despite attempts by the finance ministry to walk back Levy's comments.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it would start rolling over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps that expire in March, indicating it intended to keep a steady supply of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedge to investors next month. (Writing by Brad Haynes; Editing by Peter Galloway) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-2-3T21:55:35.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150203eb2300p8k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
