\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Weakens as  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Cuts  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2015\par 15:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:29 EST - The Brazilian real weakens nearly 2% topping 2.70 per dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  points to less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Earlier this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it will continue to auction \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts until at least the end of March 31, 2015, however from now it will offer as much as $100M in contracts in auctions held Monday through Friday. Previously, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered $200M per day in contracts. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:56 EST - Treasurys bonds are kicking off 2015 lower, with the 10-year note yielding 2.191% on the first trading day of the year, according to Tradeweb data, vs 2.173% on Wednesday afternoon. "We're reversing the unusual strength that we got on Tuesday and Wednesday," says Jim Vogel, rate strategist at FTN Financial. Mr. Vogel has his eye on ISM's December manufacturing index due out at 10:00am. (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:53 EST - Canadian dollar just reached levels not seen since July 2009, when it was recovering from the severe bruising during the global financial crisis, with USD hitting a session high at 1.1696. It doesn't reflect any sudden, new developments for the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just continued pressure from lower oil and a broadly stronger US dollar. That's reflected in the fact that CAD is outperforming its two commodity dollar compatriots -- the Australian and New Zealand dollars, the former by a narrow margin and the latter by a big one. CAD's retreat seems destined to continue, at least near term, and it'll likely keep rolling through fresh five and a half year highs. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 1340 GMT European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  is starting the New Year where the ECB left off in late 2014: warning in a German newspaper interview about the risks of too-low inflation and emphasizing that the ECB is considering fresh stimulus measures to combat them. This will do little to alter the market's expectations that the ECB will launch government bond purchases as soon as the next monetary policy meeting Jan. 22, although Mr. Draghi said it is "difficult to say" how large such a program might be. Given the weakening of the euro, which is one of the few channels the ECB has to boost growth and inflation, it's hard to imagine how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could change course now. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1325 GMT European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  president \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2 's remarks on falling mid-term inflation expectations have fanned hopes quantitative easing by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will come sooner rather than later. This in turn has pushed the euro to its lowest level in four years versus the dollar. The next focus for markets will be eurozone inflation data Wednesday, likely to show consumer prices slipping into negative territory, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ralph Solveen\f28 \uc2 \f2  at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Even if the decline is wholly due to lower oil prices, and even if core inflation actually edges higher, doves on the governing council will use the data to argue for more expansionary measures, he adds. (nikki.houston@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1257 GMT Those investors waking up in the U.S. to more headlines from ECB President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  seemingly hinting at more monetary policy largesse may be surprised at the lack of reaction from European markets. Mr. Draghi stated in an interview with Handelsblatt that "the risk we do not fulfil our mandate of price stability is higher than six months ago...We are in technical preparations to adjust the size, speed and compositions of our measures early 2015." Still, core European stock markets are between 0.2% and 0.6% lower, and the euro is marginally lower against the dollar at $1.2043. These comments are nothing new. Markets are firmly focussed upon the next ECB meeting Jan. 22 and expecting something that approximates to sovereign QE to be announced. (peter.nurse@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1257 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Higher yielding peripheral eurozone bonds start 2015 in a bullish mood after ECB President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2 's recent commentary helps stoke the QE fire. In the 10-year sector, Spain and Italy are between 5-8 bps lower at new euro-era lows of 1.545% and 1.795% respectively, while Portugal has a new low of 2.465%, down around 20 bps. Earlier Mr. Draghi signalled that the ECB is ready to launch government bond buying as the risks of deflation in the eurozone have increased. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT Despite the month-on-month increase of Brazil's manufacturing activity in December versus November, the picture for the fourth quarter regarding the country's industry was negative. The HSBC Brazil Purchasing Managers' Index average for the fourth quarter totaled 49.2 points, versus 49.6 points in the third quarter when the country posted an expansion--albeit mediocre--enough to represent recovery from the first-half recession. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1234 GMT Sterling sank to a 16-month low of $1.5414 Friday following a disappointing December manufacturing PMI reading of 52.5, down from 53.3 seen in November. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2 's short-term \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fair value model is calling sterling at $1.5289 and expects next Tuesday's services PMI to produce a downside surprise. This will keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  vulnerable ahead of Thursday's Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee meeting announcement, although this should be a non- event with rates and quantitative easing expected to be left on hold. Minutes of the meeting are released January 21. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1147 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The German electorate's main concern at the end of 2014 was immigration, according to Germany's Forsa polling group. 38% of people questioned in a survey for media outlets Stern and RTL named immigration as a problem. "Citizens attribute [to immigration] the inept distribution of asylum-seekers, social benefit abuse or the supposed high number of refugees coming to Germany," said Manfred Gullner, head of the Berlin-based institute. The poll of 2,504 conducted from 15 to 19 December gave a margin of error of plus or minus 2.5 percentage points. (harriet.torry@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1142 GMT A weaker euro, lower oil prices and less tight fiscal policies in the euro area will help fuel the Czech Republic's industrial output in 2015, despite some slowdown in the country's manufacturing in December, says Radomir Jac, chief economist at Generali PPF Asset Management. "The situation in the eurozone will remain a crucial factor for the Czech industry," he says. The latest Czech PMI reading slowed but remained in expansionary territory at 53.3 points from 56.6 points in November. The headline indicator "continues to be robust when compared both with the eurozone and the rest of the Central European region," Jac says. (leos.rousek@wsj.com, @LeoRousek) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1126 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  credit strategists expect investment grade euro bond issuance to hit EUR550bn in 2015, broadly in-line with 2014 which was the biggest year since 2009. Sterling investment grade issuance, they say, will hit GBP45bn, up 12% from 2014 levels. (josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 02, 2015 09:29 ET (14:29 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150102eb12000yr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
