\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Weakens as  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Cuts  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2015\par 15:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:29 EST - The Brazilian real weakens nearly 2% topping 2.70 per dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  points to less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Earlier this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it will continue to auction \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts until at least the end of March 31, 2015, however from now it will offer as much as $100M in contracts in auctions held Monday through Friday. Previously, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered $200M per day in contracts. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:04 EST - \uc2 Linn Energy\uc2  (LINE) said Friday it would slash its payout to shareholders by 57%, the first time it has made such a cut since going public in 2006. The oil-and-gas producer is bracing for oil prices to hover around $60 a barrel in 2015, and also plans to cut its annual capital spending to $730 million this year from $1.55 billion in 2014. LINE is the largest oil-and-gas producer of its kind, a partnership that avoids corporate taxes by distributing its leftover cash flow to shareholders, and the first to cut its payout since oil prices began falling last summer. Shares off 6.9% at $9.43 premarket, after earlier falling more than 10%. (daniel.gilbert@wsj.com; @WSJGilbert)\par \par \uc2 8:56 EST - Treasurys bonds are kicking off 2015 lower, with the 10-year note yielding 2.191% on the first trading day of the year, according to Tradeweb data, vs 2.173% on Wednesday afternoon. "We're reversing the unusual strength that we got on Tuesday and Wednesday," says Jim Vogel, rate strategist at FTN Financial. Mr. Vogel has his eye on ISM's December manufacturing index due out at 10:00am. (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:53 EST - Canadian dollar just reached levels not seen since July 2009, when it was recovering from the severe bruising during the global financial crisis, with USD hitting a session high at 1.1696. It doesn't reflect any sudden, new developments for the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just continued pressure from lower oil and a broadly stronger US dollar. That's reflected in the fact that CAD is outperforming its two commodity dollar compatriots -- the Australian and New Zealand dollars, the former by a narrow margin and the latter by a big one. CAD's retreat seems destined to continue, at least near term, and it'll likely keep rolling through fresh five and a half year highs. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 7:46 EST - In what is likely to be a common theme this year if oil prices remain near these levels, \uc2 Linn Energy\uc2  (LINE) plans to cut spending on oil and gas by 53% this year and also reduces is annual distribution by more than half. Company plans to pay $1.25 per share down from $2.90 previously, a move widely predicted by analysts, though more than some thought. Linn also says it signed a letter of intent with the private capital arm of \uc2 Blackstone Group\uc2  (BX) for $500M to fund drilling programs. LINE drops 11% premarket, extending the 67% drop over the last 12 months. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EST - U.S. stock futures point to a strong start to the year following another year of gains in 2014. Reports on U.S. manufacturing and construction spending at 10 a.m. ET may help show how the economy closed out the year and set the tone for the rest of the session. European markets trade lower following weaker-than-expected eurozone manufacturing data while the ICE dollar index rose to its highest level in close to nine years. Crude oil reverses earlier gains, while Treasury prices slip, with the 10-year yield rising back to 2.20%. S&P 500 futures climb 7 points. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 02, 2015 09:29 ET (14:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150102eb1200149\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
