\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Colombian peso, Mexican stocks skyrocket on Fed, oil\b0\par\par\uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2014\par 20:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates prices, adds Mexico markets, trader quote)\par     By Asher Levine and Jean Luis Arce\par     SAO PAULO/MEXICO CITY, Dec 18 (Reuters) - Colombia's peso\par closed sharply stronger on Thursday while Mexican stocks posted\par their biggest gain in over four years as more stable oil prices\par and reassuring comments by the U.S. Federal Reserve stoked risk\par appetite. \par     Other Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also strengthened after the\par Fed offered an upbeat assessment of the U.S. economy on\par Wednesday, helping offset mounting concerns over the recent\par decline in oil prices and a potential financial crisis in\par Russia.\par     Oil prices rebounded from five-year lows early in the\par session, though began to retreat in afternoon trading.\par     That swing was not enough to dampen a rally in Colombia's\par peso, which posted its biggest gain in nearly four years,\par strengthening to 2,317 per dollar, a gain of around 3.4 percent\par from Wednesday's close.\par     Fuel makes up about 69 percent of Colombia's merchandise\par exports, according to 2013 \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  data.\par     "This has to do with oil prices and the Fed, it's all\par helping reduce risk aversion," said Sebasti\u225\'00\'E1n D\u237\'00\'EDaz, an analyst\par with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco de Bogot\u225\'00\'E1\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  signaled on Wednesday that it would take a\par slow-going approach to rate hikes despite the strengthening U.S.\par economy, reducing concerns in Latin American markets over a\par sharper or sooner-than-expected interest rate rise.\par     The Brazilian real strengthened for a second straight\par session, rising nearly 2 percent against the dollar after\par hitting its weakest level in nearly 10 years on Tuesday.\par     Traders continued to cite risks, however, over the future of\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program, which\par is due to be modified in 2015. The bank has helped support the\par real by providing hedging protection to local businesses via\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps, though it remains unclear how the program will\par be scaled down next year.\par     Equities markets moved higher, with the MSCI Latin American\par stock index up 2 percent.\par     Mexico's IPC index was set to close with its biggest\par one-day gain in over four years as traders reacted to oil prices\par and the Fed.\par     "We also saw some bargain-hunting at very good prices, which\par helped make the rebound a bit stronger here than in other\par markets," said Carlos Alonso, a trader at Interacciones in\par Mexico City.\par     \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1924 GMT:\par      Stock indexes                     daily %    YTD %\par                           Latest       change     change\par  MSCI Emerging Markets   934.85        1.82       -8.43\par                                                   \par  MSCI LatAm              2666.15       2.08       -18.4\par                                                   \par  Brazil Bovespa          48495.7       -0.45      -5.85\par                                                   \par  Mexico IPC              42375.27      3.34       -0.82\par                                                   \par  Chile IPSA              3802.88       0.63       2.80\par                                                   \pa