\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil stocks rise on investor hope for pro-market finmin\b0\par\par\uc2 452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2014\par 17:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MEXICO CITY, Nov 21 (Reuters) - Brazilian stocks boosted\par Latin American markets on Friday, surging to a three-week high\par amid investor speculation the government will name a\par market-friendly finance minister to juice up stagnant growth. \par     The Bovespa rose 2.1 percent to 54,508.70 as reports\par speculated that President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dilma Rousseff\f28 \uc2 \f2  was considering a\par private sector executive for the post. The move would signal a\par shift away from the leftist, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies that many\par blame for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weak economic growth in recent years.\par     Respected Brazilian banker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joaquim Levy\f28 \uc2 \f2  is among three\par finalists to become the country's next finance minister, and\par Rousseff could announce her pick later Friday, a government\par official told Reuters on Friday. \par     Brazil's real also rallied, gaining 1.88 percent to\par 2.5246 per U.S. dollar as investors hailed the decision of\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut its benchmark interest rate for the\par first time in more than two years to boost its cooling economy.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it was cutting one-year\par benchmark lending rates by 40 basis points to 5.6 percent. China\par is a top customer for Brazilian raw materials. \par     China's move came after \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  head Mario\par Draghi said "excessively low" inflation had to be raised quickly\par by whatever means necessary, kindling expectations the ECB will\par move to stimulate the euro zone economy.\par     Mexico's peso gained 0.32 percent to 13.6065 per\par dollar, despite data on Friday that showed the economy slowed in\par the third quarter on weak domestic demand and uneven industrial\par output. \par     \par     Latin American stocks and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1630 GMT:\par     \par  Stock indexes                    daily %     YTD %\par                        Latest      change    change\par  MSCI LatAm           3,095.87        3.1     -6.19\par                                            \par                                            \par  Brazil Bovespa      54,695.37       2.42      6.19\par                                            \par  Mexico \f28 \uc2 \f2 IPC\f28 \uc2 \f2           44,887.77       1.53      5.06\par                                            \par  Chile IPSA           4,001.99       0.65      8.19\par                                            \par  Chile IGPA          19,473.45       0.63      6.84\par                                            \par  Argentina MerVal     9,934.05        2.7     84.27\par                                            \par  Colombia IGBC       12,965.59       0.85     -0.81\par                                            \par  Peru IGRA           15,309.54       0.81     -2.82\par                                            \par  Venezuela IBC        2,908.08       0.39      6.27\par                                            \par                                                    \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                        daily %     YTD %\par                                    change    change\par                         Latest             \par  Brazil real            2.5236       1.88     -6.61\par                                            \par  Mexico peso  