\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2 , Shares Plummet on Rousseff Re-Election -- 2nd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rogerio Jelmayer And Nicole Hong   \par \uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2014\par 15:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 S\u195\'00\'C3O PAULO--Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks plunged Monday as investors worried that the re-election of President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  would extend a period of economic stagnation.\par \par \uc2 Investors had been buying Brazilian assets for weeks on the prospect that Ms. Rousseff would lose to a more business-friendly candidate. Analysts say her narrow victory over A\u233\'00\'E9cio Neves reduces the likelihood Brazil's government will pursue policies aimed at pulling the country's economy out of a prolonged slump. Latin America's largest economy is expected to grow by less than 0.5% this year and expanded by just 2.5% last year.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rousseff faces the tough challenge of keeping Brazil out of a recession at a time when inflation is high and business confidence is low. Investors and analysts are questioning whether Ms. Rousseff has enough political support to push through tough reforms, such as simplifying tax laws and cutting subsidies for electricity and gasoline. A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could complicate the situation by making imports more expensive, adding to inflation that, at an annualized rate of 6.75%, is at its highest since October 2011.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real sank as low as 2.56 per dollar and recently traded at 2.522, on track to end the day at its lowest in nearly a decade. Brazil's Ibovespa--the main stock market index--was down nearly 4% at a six-month low.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say credit rating firms may downgrade Brazil's debt into "junk" territory if Ms. Rousseff fails to change course in her second term. Such a move would force many investors to sell bonds, leading to higher borrowing costs and putting renewed pressure on the economy. \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  Ratings Services downgraded Brazil to one notch above junk status earlier this year, but with a stable outlook, meaning a downgrade isn't imminent. Moody's Investors Service and \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  have Brazil two rungs above junk.\par \par \uc2 "We expect a very negative market reaction to the election's result as investors are likely to question the president's willingness to cut spending decisively enough to avoid a rating downgrade next year," said Luciano Rostagno, chief Latin America strategist at Banco Mizuho based in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rostagno added that Ms. Rousseff could calm markets by quickly naming a new economic team. The most immediate task for Ms. Rousseff is to name a finance minister who can reassure investors and the ratings firms that the country's fiscal situation is under control, Nomura says.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  say \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely increase its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stabilize the real.\par \par \uc2 The reaction in the bond market was more muted on Monday, with Brazilian government bonds edging slightly lower, according to traders. Analysts at Scotiabank recommended using Monday's selloff as an opportunity to buy Brazilian bonds denominated in real, given the likelihood of default in Brazil is still extremely low. Brazil's benchmark interest rate of 11% also means the country offers some of the highest yields in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The volatility in Brazilian stocks in the past few months has raised the attention of prominent hedge-fund managers. James Chanos, who founded \uc2 Kynikos Associates LP\uc2  and is famous for his bearish market bets, said in a Bloomberg Television interview last week that Brazilian stocks were like ping-pong balls moving every direction, depending on how Ms. Rousseff was faring in the election polls.\par \par \uc2 Write to Rogerio Jelmayer at rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com and Nicole Hong at nicole.hong@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 27, 2014 10:43 ET (14:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141027eaar0024s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
