\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Forex Interventions \highlight\uc2  Seen Persisting -- Market talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2014\par 14:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:21 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next administration will be obligated to continue with \b \highlight22\uc2 forex central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a sharp depreciation of the real versus the dollar, says Sidnei Nehme at the NGO \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brokerage in Sao Paulo, as economic fundamentals point to a weaker real. Neves' economic advisers have said that if elected president, he would immediately end \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  via swaps. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:16 EDT - \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2 , which had called for a flat 2014 for the S&P 500 at about 1850 before August's jump to 2300, now admits it "jumped the gun" with the call. Hoping it didn't "jump the shark" with the upward revision, the investment bank still expects 2,300 "in a reasonable horizon, with more gains as 2015 progresses." It will reassess things "once the November/December rally that we expect takes hold. Even as EPS growth slows into 2015, as we expect, we see low risk-free rates and accommodative policy as a bullish backdrop, with more countries likely to adopt proactive fiscal and monetary policies while benefiting from actions already taken." S&P 500 futures are down 13.5 points; the index closed yesterday at 1862. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 8:12 EDT - Increasing use of \uc2 Google\uc2  Glass in hospitals and industrial settings suggest wearable computers have a future in business. FinancialForce.com, a startup that makes business software that runs in the cloud, believes that such devices will have uses in the office, too. The company has built business applications that enable employees to authorize purchases and replace staff via Glass and smartwatches, such as \uc2 Samsung\uc2 's Galaxy Gear Live. Although he admits it's early days in the wearable market, FinancialForce.com CEO Jeremy Roche said that it is important to begin building such software so that if wearables gain traction, "we're not sitting behind the curve." (clint.boulton@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:07 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  (GS) has set aside 40% of its revenue during the first nine months of 2014 for employee pay and benefits. In this same period a year ago, the firm was on pace for a so-called compensation ratio of 41%. The difference? Revenue has been growing at a faster clip than compensation costs. These numbers won't be finalized until the end of year, when Goldman decides how much to pay its employees in annual bonuses. And typically, the firm's comp ratio drops during the last three months of the year. In 2013, the ratio stood at 37%. GS slips 2.6% premarket. (justin.baer@wsj.com; @Justinbaer)\par \par \uc2 7:53 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' (GS) bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders bounced back last quarter as revenue from fixed-income, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodities trading jumped 74% from a year earlier, when results were crimped by a dearth of client activity and a loss-making trade in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  business. In 3Q, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 --along with commodities and securities tied to both interest rates and mortgages--led the way. Meanwhile, revenue from credit trading, which includes corporate bonds, fell "significantly." GS is now down 2.3% premarket at $173.25 despite its big EPS beat. (justin.baer@wsj.com; @Justinbaer)\par \par \uc2 7:51 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  (GS) posts stronger-than-expected earnings in 3Q, but cuts back on the portion of revenue it sets aside for pay. GS says it set aside $2.8B for compensation and benefits in the quarter, lowering the ratio of compensation to net revenue for the first nine months of the year to 40%, compared with 43% for the first half. For the first half of 2013 GS set aside 41%. Some of that difference will go directly to shareholders as the bank hikes its dividend to 60c from 55c. GS also buys back 7.1 million shares for $1.25B. GS down 2% premarket amid a broad market slide. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:49 EDT - While the US dollar spiked to RBC's C$1.1350 profit target yesterday and it's sticking with its C$1.15 year-end objective, waiting for a pullback to C$1.11-1.12 is RBC's favored strategy right now instead of piling back in at current elevated levels. Meanwhile, RBC says NZD/USD looks like a good sell at current levels given the high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . RBC also calls USD/JPY still the most-highly correlated pair to equity returns, so if risk aversion persists, another clearout of stale longs may be seen. Lastly, the investment bank calls EUR/USD more complicated as positioning is heavily short, which argues for risk of a further unwind. But peripheral pressures are on the rise, a setup which argues for limited exposure in EUR/USD. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:42 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  (GS), perhaps unsurprisingly, smokes consensus EPS forecasts as the metric was 42% higher than Wall Street anticipated. CEO \uc2 Lloyd Blankfein\uc2  is expecting good things to come, noting, "While conditions and sentiment can shift quickly, the strength of our transaction backlog indicates our clients' desire to pursue and execute their strategic plans for growth." Investment-banking revenue jumped 26% amid \uc2 M&A\uc2  activity while FICC revenue surged 74% versus the "challenging" 3Q13. As the topline overall jumped 25%, operating expenses only increased half that and the ratio of compensation and benefits to revenue slid to 40% from 43% in 1H. GS is down 0.3% premarket to $176.74, holding up better than the broader market. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EDT - After rebounding some an hour ago, US stock-index futures have slid back, with Dow-industrial futures off 150. There's nothing new on the news front--just another bout of profit-taking following late Wednesday's equity rebound in the US. That reversal helped erase nearly all of Treasurys' early gains, but that market is back in rally mode this morning with the 10-year yield down to an even 2%. Meanwhile, oil falls anews, with \uc2 Nymex\uc2  crude off 2% and making a fresh run at breaching $80. The dollar is broadly higher, with the euro pulling back to $1.2750. Gold is down slightly. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:20 EDT - A compensation surge helps result in \uc2 Blackstone\uc2 's (BX) core 3Q EPS falling short of consensus views as the private-equity giant reaps the rewards from past investments. In private equity, compensation more than tripled thanks to $124.4M of performance-fee compensation amid fund appreciation and $14.7B of asset sales the past year. BX notes net accrued performance fees has grown at a compound annual rate of 45% the past 2 years amid "continued appreciation in real estate and private equity." The figure was $4.3B as of last quarter, and in the past year, $1.54B of performance fees have been realized. BX, inactive premarket, is down 7.7% this month. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:16 EDT - Wall Street shares finished well above yesterday's lows, but European equities took little comfort as they were hit by a fresh round of selling. Overall, there is a deep sense of concern that investor confidence has dried up, creating an environment in which news that would otherwise be ignored can trigger selling. One example is the Ebola outbreak, as the actual, direct impact on the developed world is likely to be very small. The market is fearing this fear. Meanwhile, in realm of more realistic risks, global deflation is rearing its ugly head. Concerns about that threat were reinforced by a weak eurozone inflation print for September. And peripheral eurozone sovereign bond yields started to rocket again--those on Greek 10-year bonds shot up to 8.8% from 6.6% a couple of weeks ago. (alen.mattich@wsj.com and michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:11 EDT - A strong 3Q beat, especially on the profit side, for Snap-on (SNA) as the auto-tools maker continue handily outpaces consensus views. SNA has been posting solid growth for some time now, sending shares essentially straight up for 3 straight years before the past month's 10% pullback. Today's results should soothe some investors' concerns today despite another broad pullback in stocks poised to occur. SNA is inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 16, 2014 08:21 ET (12:21 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141016eaag001qo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
